WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.741
This is module 3, organizing and
converting. Before you

00:00:04.741 --> 00:00:06.120
process a single

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.716
file in GGIR, you have to get your data
organized. In this

00:00:09.716 --> 00:00:11.080
module, you'll build a

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.515
folder system where every accelerometer
file traces back to

00:00:14.515 --> 00:00:15.680
the participant, the

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.308
batch, and the decisions that produced it.
Let's set it up.

00:00:20.308 --> 00:00:21.720
Here's what you'll

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.343
walk away able to do. First, build a
folder structure that

00:00:25.343 --> 00:00:26.280
keeps your raw,

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.674
converted, processed, and final files
clearly separated.

00:00:30.674 --> 00:00:32.400
Second, name files and

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.030
keep batch logs in a way that stays
consistent across the

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:37.240
whole study. Third,

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.811
convert ActiGraph files in ActiLife, but
only when you

00:00:40.811 --> 00:00:42.200
actually need to. And

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.782
fourth, explain why converting from GT3X
to CSV depends on

00:00:46.782 --> 00:00:48.520
your pipeline, instead

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.654
of being something everyone has to do.
That last one trips

00:00:51.654 --> 00:00:52.680
people up, so we'll

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.267
come back to it. Begin by separating raw
evidence from every

00:00:57.267 --> 00:00:58.720
file created later.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.256
Keep each download batch inside
01_raw_downloads with complete

00:01:03.256 --> 00:01:04.280
and incomplete

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.270
used only as preliminary triage labels.
They do not indicate

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:09.400
final participant

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.726
validity. After verification and handoff,
treat raw files as

00:01:13.726 --> 00:01:14.880
read-only. Store

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.849
converted files, GGIR inputs and outputs,
quality control

00:01:18.849 --> 00:01:20.520
files, clean data, final

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.288
data sets, and logs in separate folders.
If an approved rename

00:01:24.288 --> 00:01:25.200
is unavoidable,

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.792
preserve the original to new file name
mapping in the batch

00:01:29.792 --> 00:01:30.960
log. It's worth

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.304
being clear about why raw gets special
treatment. Look at the

00:01:34.304 --> 00:01:35.400
pipeline end-to-end.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:41.818
Raw, converted, GGIR input, GGIR output,
QC, cleaned, and

00:01:41.818 --> 00:01:44.520
final. Every stage after

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.180
raw is regenerable. If your clean dataset
gets corrupted, you

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.020
can rebuild it

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.355
from the raw files and your scripts. But
the raw download is

00:01:52.355 --> 00:01:53.300
the one thing you

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.777
can't recreate. The participant already
gave their week back

00:01:56.777 --> 00:01:57.820
and the device has

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.715
been wiped and sent out again. Lose a raw
file and nothing

00:02:01.715 --> 00:02:03.260
downstream fixes it, so

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.480
the whole point of organizing carefully is
to protect the

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:07.780
files you can't remake.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:13.240
Use a coded, de-identified study ID, never
a participant's

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.500
name. Six things must be

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:19.677
recoverable from the file name and batch
log. Coded ID, device

00:02:19.677 --> 00:02:20.620
serial number,

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:24.681
collection batch, download date, operator,
and file format and

00:02:24.681 --> 00:02:25.860
processing status.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.743
Also log the original file name, source
folder, ActiLife

00:02:29.743 --> 00:02:31.060
version, conversion

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:34.643
settings, date, and outcome. If a raw file
must be renamed,

00:02:34.643 --> 00:02:36.100
preserve the original to

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:42.976
new filename mapping. Avoid names like
participant1.csv,

00:02:42.976 --> 00:02:44.940
newfile.gt3x, or

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:49.361
final_final_version2. They tell you
nothing: which

00:02:49.361 --> 00:02:51.660
participant, which device,

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.725
which batch. Once the files are mixed
together, that

00:02:54.725 --> 00:02:57.260
information is gone. Instead, use a
pattern

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.501
that carries the information in the name
itself. Study ID,

00:03:01.501 --> 00:03:04.060
then batch, then the device serial,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:08.565
then the download date, then the status,
raw, and the format,

00:03:08.565 --> 00:03:10.780
GT3X. When you convert a file,

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:14.598
the converted copy follows the same
pattern, but ends in

00:03:14.598 --> 00:03:16.780
converted.csv. The exact pattern

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.947
can differ between studies. What matters
is that someone else

00:03:19.947 --> 00:03:21.660
can decode it without asking you.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.561
Before converting anything, make a
pipeline decision. GGIR can

00:03:26.561 --> 00:03:28.300
read compatible modern

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:32.752
GT3X files directly, so CSV conversion is
not automatically

00:03:32.752 --> 00:03:34.940
required. Convert only when a

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:38.440
downstream script, external application,
sharing requirement,

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.620
or approved audit process specifically

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:45.401
needs CSV. If conversion is required, use
batch raw-to-raw

00:03:45.401 --> 00:03:48.220
conversion, retain the original GT3X

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:51.864
files, and remember that this operation
preserves the sampling

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:53.980
frequency. It does not create epochs

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:57.916
or activity counts. The next clips show a
legacy ActiLife

00:03:57.916 --> 00:04:00.540
interface. Labels may vary by version.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:07.547
This recording uses a legacy ActiLife
interface. Open file,

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:10.220
choose import, export, convert,

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.826
open raw-to-raw, and select GT3X to CSV
for batch conversion.

00:04:14.826 --> 00:04:16.940
Add the directory containing

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:21.548
the eligible GT3X files instead of
exporting files one at a

00:04:21.548 --> 00:04:23.500
time. Only GT3X files are

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:28.613
conversion inputs. Paired AGD summary
files are not converted.

00:04:28.613 --> 00:04:31.500
Use add directory for a whole batch

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:36.601
or Add Dataset for individual GT3X files.
For this course's

00:04:36.601 --> 00:04:40.060
GGIR CSV workflow, leave TimeStamps off,

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:43.471
keep column headers on, and save outputs
in a separate

00:04:43.471 --> 00:04:46.060
access-controlled converted files folder,

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:51.020
not in the raw download folder. Confirm
that the eligible GT3X

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.260
count matches the batch log,

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.079
then start the export. Record any
warnings, skipped files, or

00:04:57.079 --> 00:04:59.020
failures in the conversion log.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:06.753
After export, verify one non-empty CSV for
every eligible GT3X

00:05:06.753 --> 00:05:09.980
input. Count GT3X and CSV files

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:14.854
separately. The original GT3X files
remain. Inspect the CSV

00:05:14.854 --> 00:05:17.580
header with R, Python, or another

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:21.864
text-based tool. Do not use Excel to
validate a complete raw

00:05:21.864 --> 00:05:24.220
CSV because a full high-frequency

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:28.160
file may exceed spreadsheet limits.
Reconcile missing,

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.860
duplicate, skipped, or failed outputs

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:36.415
before processing. Do you need to convert?
Often no. GGIR can

00:05:36.415 --> 00:05:39.420
read compatible modern GT3X files

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:43.825
directly. ActiGraph serial numbers
beginning NEO or MRA with

00:05:43.825 --> 00:05:45.660
firmware 2.5.0 or earlier

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.149
use an older GT3X format and may require a
validated

00:05:48.149 --> 00:05:51.020
alternative workflow. Check device and
firmware information,

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:55.060
confirm compatibility with the GGIR
version in use, and test

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.820
one file before releasing the full batch.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:01.623
Convert only when your approved pipeline
specifically requires

00:06:01.623 --> 00:06:03.340
CSV and document the reason.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:07.863
One last safeguard. Define the eligible
inputs before

00:06:07.863 --> 00:06:11.020
comparing counts. If a batch contains

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:16.224
N eligible GT3X files and all convert
successfully, expect N

00:06:16.224 --> 00:06:19.260
non-empty CSV outputs. A folder may

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:24.567
contain twice as many total files when
each coded record has a

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:27.820
GT3X/AGD pair, but only the GT3X files

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:31.853
are conversion inputs. If the eligible
input and successful

00:06:31.853 --> 00:06:33.260
output counts differ,

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:38.083
stop and reconcile the conversion log
before processing. To

00:06:38.083 --> 00:06:40.780
recap, separate raw evidence from

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:45.079
derived files, use coded IDs and
consistent batch logs, and

00:06:45.079 --> 00:06:48.140
treat verified raw downloads as read-only.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:52.601
Convert only when the approved pipeline
requires CSV. In the

00:06:52.601 --> 00:06:54.460
legacy ActiLife workflow,

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:58.335
use batch raw-to-raw conversion. Keep
timestamps off and

00:06:58.335 --> 00:06:59.580
column headers on,

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:03.335
write to a separate converted folder, and
verify that N

00:07:03.335 --> 00:07:04.700
eligible GT3X inputs

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:08.940
produce N successful CSV outputs before
continuing.
